  module: "Module 4: Connecting Accelerators into a Cluster",
  title: "Reliability and Synchronization — Why One Slow Component Can Stall the Cluster",
  date: "2026-09-22",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 042: A faster accelerator lowers cost only when the time it saves is worth more than its higher hourly price.

Lesson 043: Scale-up tightly connects nearby accelerators, while scale-out connects servers and racks into a larger cluster.

Lesson 044: Collective operations combine or share results from many accelerators, so one delayed participant can make the group wait.

Lesson 045: Network interfaces and switches move data between servers, but shared connections can slow several transfers at once.

Lesson 046: Copper is efficient over short connections, while optics adds hardware and power to carry signals reliably over longer distances.

The big idea

When a training job needs results from every worker before continuing, one slow worker can hold up the group.

A worker is a running copy of the training program, often assigned to one GPU. Synchronization means workers coordinate at required points so they use consistent results.

Even if 1,023 GPUs are healthy, a job requiring all 1,024 may stop when one worker fails. Reliability therefore means keeping the job making progress, as well as keeping individual parts working.

1. Why a small delay spreads

In synchronous training, workers process different pieces of a batch: a group of training examples. They calculate gradients, the numbers used to adjust the model's weights.

A collective combines their results. Each worker needs the combined answer before completing the model update and moving on.

This creates a barrier: a point beyond which work cannot proceed until the required participants have arrived. Communication can overlap some computation, but the dependent work still needs its result.

Suppose most workers finish their calculations in 900 milliseconds and one takes 1,100 milliseconds. The others can spend roughly 200 milliseconds waiting for it.

That delayed worker is a straggler. It might have more work, insufficient memory, an overheated GPU that slows itself down, a software interruption or a network transfer that needs retrying.

Average speed can hide this problem. Tail latency means the unusually long delays near the slow end of the results. When everyone needs the last answer, those delays matter.

2. Distinguish slow, stuck and failed

A slowdown still makes progress, just later than expected. Measure each worker's calculation and communication times to find the outlier.

A hang means no visible progress. For example, one worker may call a different collective operation from the others. They wait for an exchange that cannot complete as expected. Sending differently sized data arrays can also cause a mismatch.

Each worker has an identifying number called a rank. Per-rank diagnostics simply means examining workers individually rather than looking only at a cluster average.

A timeout flags an operation that has taken too long. PyTorch's monitored barrier can report which ranks did not acknowledge a waiting point in time. NVIDIA's NCCL communication library also has a RAS subsystem—reliability, availability and serviceability—which helps identify unresponsive processes.

A hard failure means a participant is no longer available: a program crashes, a GPU fails or a server loses power. Recovery may require stopping the current exchange, replacing the worker and restarting the group.

These are separate jobs: notice the problem, find its cause, then restore the training job. Detecting a failure quickly does not by itself recover the work.

3. A checkpoint is a saved restart point

A checkpoint stores enough training state to resume later.

